  "content": {
    "id": "p7-q8-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "8",
    "prompt": "Find all pairs of integers $x$ and $y$ such that $x^2-y^2=77$.",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [],
    "answer": {
      "short": "$(x,y)=(39,38),(39,-38),(-39,38),(-39,-38),(9,2),(9,-2),(-9,2),(-9,-2)$.\n\nFactorise as $(x-y)(x+y)=77$ and consider all signed factor pairs.",
      "worked": "$$\\begin{align*}x^2-y^2&=77\\\\(x-y)(x+y)&=77.\\end{align*}$$\nLet $u=x-y$ and $v=x+y$. The positive ordered factor pairs $(u,v)$ are $(1,77),(7,11),(11,7),(77,1)$. Both factors may also be negative, giving $(-1,-77),(-7,-11),(-11,-7),(-77,-1)$.\n$$\\begin{align*}x&=\\frac{u+v}{2},\\\\y&=\\frac{v-u}{2}.\\end{align*}$$\nThese give $(x,y)=(39,38),(9,2),(9,-2),(39,-38),(-39,-38),(-9,-2),(-9,2),(-39,38)$. Since these exhaust the integer factor pairs of 77, there are no other solutions.",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
    "answerSpaceMm": 86
  },
